{"collection_no":"151252","record_type":"col","lng":4.750000,"lat":45.866669,"collection_name":"Lissieu, Lutetian","n_occs":22,"early_interval":"Lutetian","max_ma":48.07,"min_ma":41.03,"reference_no":"49420","cc":"FR","state":"Rhone","latlng_basis":"based on nearby landmark","latlng_precision":"minutes","geogcomments":"\"The locality no longer exists. It was located in a western extension of the Mont d’Or lyonnais, a small limestone mount located 10 km north-northwest of Lyon, central eastern France (‘‘département’’, or administrative region, of Rhône). The fossils were recovered from a fissure-fill situated close to the\r\nhamlet of La Clôtre, near the village of Lissieu. The enclosing rocks, and therefore the fissure disappeared (mined out) by the end of the nineteenth century.\" - Rage and Auge (2010)","environment":"fissure fill","primary_reference":"D. Angst, E. Buffetaut, and C. Lecuyer, R. Amiot. 2013. ‘‘Terror Birds’’ (Phorusrhacidae) from the Eocene of Europe Imply Trans-Tethys Dispersal. PLoS One 8(11):e80357"},
